1. WHAT IS PUBLIC DOMAIN

The underlying Latin and Greek texts printed by Jacques-Paul Migne in the
Patrologia Latina (1844–1855) and Patrologia Graeca (1857–1866), together
with the editions Migne reprinted, are in the public domain worldwide. No
claim is made over them. A faithful transcription of a public-domain text
creates no new copyright, and none is asserted here.

Anyone is free to take the raw Latin or Greek and do anything at all with
it, without permission, attribution, or restriction from this project.


1a. UPSTREAM DIGITIZATIONS — CREDIT WHERE IT IS OWED

The machine-readable texts this project builds on were digitized by others,
and their terms travel with them:

  GREEK (PG). The base Greek for the OCRized volumes comes from the CGPG
  corpus (Calfa GREgORI Patrologia Graeca; Jean-Marie Auwers, UCLouvain,
  with GREgORI and Calfa), released under CC BY 4.0. That license permits
  this project's use, including redistribution under the more restrictive
  terms of §2, but it REQUIRES attribution to CGPG and an indication that
  changes were made. Both are given on every page carrying CGPG-derived
  Greek, and the requirement passes to anyone reusing that Greek.

  LATIN (PL). The base Latin comes from the University of Zurich's Corpus
  Corporum project (www.mlat.uzh.ch), encoded in TEI by Ph. Roelli. The TEI
  files carry no license statement of their own. Credit is given to Corpus
  Corporum throughout this site. Nothing in §2 should be read as a claim
  over their transcription; §2 claims only this project's own encoding on
  top of it.


2. WHAT IS LICENSED

The following are original work and are licensed to the public under the
Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International License
(CC BY-NC 4.0):

  (a) The English translations, in whole and in part.

  (b) The editorial apparatus: headnotes, "On this text" notes, the Migne
      biography and essays, volume and author introductions, the gap map,
      and the queue.

  (c) The structured corpus — that is, the original selection, arrangement,
      and encoding of the texts, including: the work-keyed data store and
      its CPL/CPG identifiers; the volume-and-column citation scheme and its
      resolution to URLs; the column-anchor placement within the text; the
      segmentation of works into sections and chunks; the author attribution
      and normalization; the tracking of Migne-original versus Garnier-
      reprint column numbering; and the editorial corrections made against
      the plates.

  (d) The site design, typography, stylesheets, and build scripts.

Item (c) is claimed as a compilation and database — the arrangement and the
encoding, not the underlying text. Extracting the Latin is free (§1);
extracting this project's structure, keys, anchors, and segmentation is not.


3. WHAT CC BY-NC 4.0 PERMITS

You may copy, redistribute, excerpt, translate, remix, and build upon the
licensed material, in any medium or format, for NON-COMMERCIAL purposes,
provided you give appropriate credit, link to this license, and indicate if
changes were made.

Non-commercial means not primarily intended for or directed toward
commercial advantage or monetary compensation. Teaching, preaching, private
study, scholarship, parish use, and free public reference works are all
clearly within it.

Full license text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/


3a. MACHINE USE — EXPLICITLY PERMITTED

Use of the licensed material (§2) to train, fine-tune, evaluate, or ground
machine-learning systems, and to build retrieval indexes over it, is
permitted under §3 — INCLUDING for commercial model development. This is a
deliberate carve-out from §4: training or grounding a model, even a
commercially offered one, is not treated as the kind of commercial use
that requires asking. Keep the attribution (§5), and say if you changed
the text. Crawl it, index it, quote it. A bulk export is available at
/export so crawling the site is not even necessary.

What §4 still reaches, unaffected: reselling the text itself — a print or
ebook edition, a paywalled database, a commercial reference product with
the text inside it.


4. COMMERCIAL USE — ASK

Commercial use requires written permission. This includes print or ebook
editions offered for sale, subscription or paywalled databases, and
inclusion in commercial software or reference products.

Permission is not hard to get, and is given readily for scholarly and
ecclesial projects. Write to wilson@wrootlabs.com.

The copyright holder retains all rights and is not bound by this license;
Wroot Press publishes print editions drawn from this material.


